    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d525">

                <pb facs="0721" n="631" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg631"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">300.115/20497: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-01T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 1,
                                1919—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your November 26, 6 p.m. Department believes deposits to effect release of goods
                    detained under Order in Council of March 11, 1915, should be released in proper
                    cases without further delay as economic <pb facs="0722" n="632" xml:id="pg632"/>
                    reasons for detention of goods ceased long ago. Ambassador has taken up matter
                    of release of these deposits with Foreign Office, but as yet no reply has been
                    received. Department desires to obtain list of these cases and amount deposited
                    in each case and total amount involved. Telegraph report.<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d525-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
